## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, speeds, and ranges in this specification are given in the
game's own logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`,
16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y`
increases downward.

An entity's position is its center. Wherever the ship, a drone, a bullet, or a
drone-burst is given, taken, or reported as an `(x, y)` pair, that pair is the
center of the entity rather than the top-left corner of its sprite. Every extent
this specification states as a half-extent is measured from that center.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the browser
  window is the runtime's work: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect ratio,
  the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete stage is
  therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update. `specs/simulation.md`
states how a frame is divided and in what order a division resolves;
`specs/field.md` states the geometry every position above is placed in.

## The screens the build has

The build carries seven screens, and the game is on exactly one of them at a time:
`title`, `howto`, `stageIntro`, `inWave`, `paused`, `stageCleared`, and `gameOver`.
The `inWave` screen runs in two sub-phases, `live` and `ready`. `specs/ui.md`
defines what each screen shows and where each one leads, and
`specs/progression.md` defines the phases.

## What the field shows

While a wave is being played, the play field carries every drone standing on it in
its own band, every bullet in flight, every drone-burst playing, the discharge wave
while it lasts, and the ship inside its lane. The starfield sits behind all of it.
The HUD strip above the field carries the score and the stage; the strip below
carries the lives, the resonance meter, and the polarity indicator.
`specs/field.md` gives the extent of each region and `specs/ui.md` the readouts.

## Visual design

The field is dark. Choose the colors and the typography, decide how the ship, a
drone, a bullet, the discharge, and the inversion are drawn, and decide what the
field and its HUD strips look like behind them.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| The two bands | A cyan thing and a magenta thing are told apart at a glance, and each is told apart from the field behind it. |
| One palette for both | The band colors are the two the seeded art carries. Everything the build draws in code that carries a band uses those same two colors, so a drone and a bullet of one band read as the same band. A bullet, the polarity indicator, and a band-carrying effect are all included. |
| Band by more than color | Each band carries a distinct shape accent, the ring for cyan and the diamond for magenta, used consistently everywhere a band appears, so the bands stay legible to a colorblind player. |
| The ship | The ship reads apart from the drones, and its current band is readable on the ship itself. |
| The three drones | A Shard, a Flux, and a Prism are told apart from one another. |
| A Flux's shimmer | A shimmering Flux is visibly different from a Flux holding a band. |
| A Prism's layers | A Prism with its shell intact and one with only its core left are told apart. |
| A bullet in flight | A bullet reads apart from the field along the lane it is traveling. |
| The inversion | While a spectral inversion is active the field carries an unmistakable, field-wide mark that is absent otherwise. |
| The starfield | The starfield sits behind the play field and never reads as bright as a drone of either band, so nothing on it is mistaken for something to shoot. |
| Text | Every readout and every screen's text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, the type, the glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
